# Finite-field pair packets and integer height transport

Import-only grouping of the sharp prime-pair weight laws and the complete
raw elliptic Fourier rows, quantitative native-weight separation, and global
reconstruction of actual integer prime pairs from cofinite native profiles
or adaptive observations. Arbitrary distinct prime pairs have exact native
agreement fraction three eighths or one half, with explicit integer square
tests for both full cross characters; consecutive pairs have fraction three
eighths. The existing
adaptive reconstruction thresholds are retained.
For a fixed reference pair, the exceptional half-agreement neighborhood
has an explicit square-root count in the candidate upper endpoint.
Every pairwise half-agreement family, finite or infinite, shares one lower
or one upper squareclass row. Unbounded upper-endpoint families use only
the lower row, with all lower primes dividing each fixed lower invariant.
Every fixed finite native agreement form has sharp coercivity three eighths,
including its physical limit and an exact four-pair equality witness.
An upper squareclass row fixes the upper prime endpoint; a lower row fixes
the lower prime endpoint when both compared gaps are short.
A square-factor cutoff gives a large short-pair subfamily whose distinct
agreement rates are three eighths, with an explicit finite extraction bound.
The identities retain the Gauss-Kloosterman term and its compensation. No new
integer pair-supply estimate is asserted.
